<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web">

  <!--
    The operator's window onto a running deployment, and a third binary rather than a flag on
    either of the other two.

    It exists because a fleet stopped fitting in a terminal. Twenty repositories, each with an
    agent that may or may not be attending to its mail, cannot be watched by tailing twenty
    windows - and the failure this whole project spent a week on was invisible precisely
    because nobody was watching the one window where it showed.

    Three things it is deliberately not:

    It is not required. Parley runs exactly as it does today with this process absent; the
    store is the shared state and this is one more reader of it. A bridge that had to be up
    for agents to talk would have made the observability a dependency of the thing observed.

    It is not an agent. It never sends a message, never answers a question, and takes no
    `from` parameter, because identity in this protocol comes from PARLEY_AGENT in a launch
    environment and nowhere else (SPEC.md 4.1). An operator who could speak as `docs` would
    make every provenance claim in the system unfalsifiable. It observes and it operates; it
    does not participate.

    It is not a second operator surface. Every operation it offers is one OperatorConsole
    already had, reached over HTTP instead of over argv. SeparationTests and
    SurfaceBoundaryTests keep the operator/agent split honest, and routing through the same
    type means this inherits that guard rather than quietly growing a fourteenth power.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <AssemblyName>parley-bridge</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>Parley.Bridge</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <PackAsTool>true</PackAsTool>
    <ToolCommandName>parley-bridge</ToolCommandName>
    <PackageId>Parley.Bridge</PackageId>
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Parley.Headless is referenced for the same reason Parley.Cli references it: keeping an
    agent resident is an operator capability. The bridge hosts ResidentAgent instances for
    agents it starts, which is what makes their stdio visible at all - a process's output can
    only be read by the process that spawned it, so an agent launched by hand in a terminal is
    observable through the store and no further.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Parley.Broker\Parley.Broker.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Parley.Headless\Parley.Headless.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
